A||||12||Seanh||Removing the Engine||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-12-2000||09:57 PM||||Whats the best way to remove the V6 engine from the Fiero? I am sure it will be easier to drop the entire cradle, rather then taking it out the top. I dont have an engine hoist, but could probally rent one. I was wondering, should I jack the car up and use a hoist to drop the engine down, or support the cradle and remove the bolts and pick the car up over it? I would like to pick the car up over it, but how exactly do I support the cradle on the ground? Rollaround type thing? Jack stands? Also, how should I go about jacking the car that high in the air if I decide to drop the cradle, what height jackstands will I need and where should I place them, and how the heck do you get the car that high in the air to put the jackstands under it in the first place? Please help, I have no idea how to do this. I was also wondering if Archie's video would help much? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||10-12-2000||10:31 PM||||<BR> I Will be takeing my 4-cyl out the top I don't know if the 2.8 will fit that way though. you can suport the cradle with wood blocks or a dolly of somekind. a standard engine lift should be able to pick up the rear of the car w/ no motor just fine. you will need a roll about dolly at some point to either move the cradle or car out of the others way.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-12-2000||11:07 PM||||Where would I hook the engine lift on the rear of the car?<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||10-13-2000||01:22 AM||||Some people use the trunk latch to lift up the rear of the car.  From what I hear it works pretty good.  <BR>You can also use a long 4x4 to lift the car from the spare tire jack slots/things on the side of the car, in front of the rear wheel.  If you use this method, the front wheels will come off the ground about 4-6" when you have the rear up high enough to roll the engine out from underneath.  A solution to that is to put the front tires on a block of wood or brick, to keep them on the ground.  <P>Get a pallet and screw some wheels on the bottom of it, and drop the cradle onto that.  That's how I'm planning to do it.  Good luck! <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||10-13-2000||09:38 AM||||I took mine out the bootom, but I had a hoise, made it super easy, unbolt everything, and lift car. Altho it is possible to pull the engine out the top, it'll be one heck of a learning experience. there's trade offs either way, the top can be faster with some experience (so I've heard) but the bottom is the tried and trued way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||10-13-2000||10:02 AM||||A guy near me uses a come-along, hooked to a beam in his basement. He has a chain with hooks on both ends. Hooks it into the holes in the shock towers and lifts the car up off of the cradle.<BR>I've heard that two furniture dollies side by side, with 2x4s across them, could be used to roll the engine out.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Max The Chainsaw||10-13-2000||10:39 AM||||Well, I've never done it myself, but my brother in law used to work for a GM dealer during the mid 80's, and he told me what they did was support the cradle on jackstands (didn't need to be very large ones, heck the whole car only weighs just over 1 ton!) Unhooked everything and two guys, one at each wheel well would just pick up the rear of the car (Most of the weight in in the cradle) and simply walk it forward on the front wheels.<P>Sounds a little too easy to me though...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fieroboy1||10-13-2000||07:07 PM||||I wouldn't recommend pulling on the trunk latch to lift the car...They bend way too easily.  I drill two holes on the inner wall of the trunk closest to the engine compartment.  Pull the carpet back first so you can disguise the holes later, and put two hardened bolts through and get a hoop of good strong chain.  I use a chain block from the ceiling of my garage, and i drop the motor from the bottom.<P><BR>84 v/8 4 spd (dismantled)<BR>86 gt fastback (engineless)<BR>86 se v/6 (coming soon)<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000008||Seanh||10-13-2000||08:14 PM||||I was thinking about that during school today. I was wondering if the trunk latch would really hold. I am going to do this probally next weekend, but maybe start taking the necessary things off the engine to remove it. I whiped out my Haynes manual and me being the stupid person I am, didnt even think to look in there about removing the engine. It lists all the things I have to do. I am going to build the pallat to support the engine. Any ideas on this, heights for the supports to put on it, distance apart? I am going to jack the car up and roll and pallat under it and lower it onto the pallat. I am going to rent an engine hoist and pick the car up over it. I was also thinking about what you said about two guys picking the car up over the engine. Can I do this? I could round up a couple of friends and to do it, or would it just be easier to get the hoist? What do you others have to say about the trunk latch bending? I thought about this today, and now looking at the latch, and hearing of two people picking the car up without the cradle, I think it might hold, but Im not sure. Any other things I could do without having to drill holes? Strut towers maybe? Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000009||tiacPaul||10-14-2000||03:16 AM||||I hooked the hoist to the chasis under the bumper and used a boars to protect my bumper.  That goodness for enduraflex!   I just had the engine on a cart.  worked great.  <BR>1.raise car<BR>2.slide engine out<BR>3.let car down<BR>4.fix engine and clean everything!<BR>5. lift car<BR>6.roll engine back in<BR>7.put car back on engine and go!<P>||||reg||14
Z||000010||Phil||10-14-2000||05:20 AM||||I pull the rear facia(about 10 min.) then slide a bar through the bumper support and hoist away.||||reg||1
Z||000011||862M4inCA||10-14-2000||06:37 AM||||Well, Yet once again, I have a different method I used when I replaced my motor. I went for the method most guys have mentioned by using the trunk latch to lift the car. That worked just fine, although it DID bend it a little, I just took a hammer and bent it back into place again. Held the car up for about a week like that though. Anyway, after removing the engine from under the car, I replaced the cradle back under the car, with the transaxle still on the cradle. Once I got to the point that I was ready to install the new motor, I lowered the car, tightened all the cradle mountings, and lowered the engine into place through the top. I had to remove the alternator and bracket, as well as the waterpump assembly (where it bolts to the block) to get enough clearance to drop the motor in the rest of the way. Actually went a LOT faster than I'd expected.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-14-2000||12:37 PM||||I like that idea about removing the rear bumper. I think that is the way I will go. Thanks for all the info. Also, Im going to use a pallat to support the cradle. How big and how many wheels should I use on it? I will probally not remove the engine from the pallat, just work on it there for no more then I need to do. I am going to start disconecting everthing today and will remove it next weekend, work on it that week, and put it in the following weekend.  Sound like so much fun!!!<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
